INT. LUNAR RADIO OBSERVATORY - CONTROL ROOM - DAY

A cramped steel room lit by banks of green screens. Out the porthole, a grey plain of regolith and a dish the size of a stadium tilting toward the stars. The air tastes of recycled coffee.

COMMANDER NATHANIEL ASHBY, 50s, a command jacket and a weary jaw, sits in the center seat. DR. PRIYAMVADA LUTHRA, 40s, in a lab coat, stands at the main console. MR. PETROVSKY, 60s, the old engineer, eats from a tube. MISS ELLINGTON, 30s, a communications officer with a headset. TECH DOYLE, 20s, a freckled intern, hugs a clipboard.

ASHBY
Dr. Luthra. Report.

LUTHRA
Three signals. Same source. Same frequency. Same pattern every eleven minutes.

ELLINGTON
It repeats exactly.

PETROVSKY
No natural source repeats exactly.

DOYLE
Could it be a pulsar?

LUTHRA
Pulsars drift. This doesn't.

ASHBY
Origin?

LUTHRA
Sagittarius. Forty light years.

PETROVSKY
So whoever sent it, sent it forty years ago.

ELLINGTON
Or they're still sending.

DOYLE
Does it say anything?

LUTHRA
It's a prime sequence. Two, three, five, seven, eleven. Then a gap. Then --

ASHBY
Then?

LUTHRA
Then our own coordinates.

Silence. The dish groans outside, a long metallic sound.

PETROVSKY
Our coordinates. The Moon's.

LUTHRA
The Moon's. Down to the meter.

ELLINGTON
Somebody knows we're here.

DOYLE
Or somebody always did.

Ashby stands. Looks at the screens. Looks at the porthole. The dish turns another degree.

ASHBY
Send nothing. Log everything. Nobody leaves this room.
